// app:graph — the neutral scene graph: parent/child, composed
// Node2D transforms, lifecycle by tree walk, in ordinary program memory.
//
//     import "app:graph"
//
//     appgraph.Graph g = graph.init() using app.allocator()
//     appgraph.Node player = graph.create(&g, position: (10.0, 5.0))
//     graph.set_on_process(&g, player, on_player_process)
//     appgraph.Node weapon = graph.create(&g, position: (1.0, 0.0))
//     graph.add_child(&g, appgraph.INVALID, player)   // a root
//     graph.add_child(&g, player, weapon)             // rides along with the player
//
//     void process(f32 dt):
//         graph.process(&g, dt)                        // walks the tree, fires on_process
//         [2]f32 muzzle = graph.to_global(&g, weapon, (0.5, 0.0))
//
// ── Neutral, not graphical ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A headless server builds this same tree, positions nodes and runs their
// `process` hook — it just never draws them. Drawing a node is
// a presentation layer's job, gated on `VectorDraw`; nothing here imports a
// backend or knows one exists.
//
// ── An explicit value, not a singleton ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// `app:log`/`app:input`/`app:net` are process-wide singletons because what
// they wrap genuinely has one instance per program (one keyboard, one log
// stream, one network host). A scene graph does not — it is app-defined
// state, the same way `app:ui`'s `UIGraph` is, and the two are drawn as
// trees as siblings on purpose. So `Graph` is a value the caller creates
// with `init` and passes explicitly to every call here, exactly like
// `app:ui`'s `^UIGraph` parameter, not a package-level global.
//
// ── Handles, not pointers (`Node` is `(i32 index, u32 generation)`) ────────
//
// Nodes live in one growable `ref []Slot` the `Graph` owns. A `ref` can
// move when it grows (`resize`), so a raw `^Slot` a caller held across a
// `create`/`add_child`/`remove` call could dangle — the same class of bug
// `app:events` hit for a `rawptr` held past its pointee's lifetime, before
// it gave way to typed channels.
// A `Node` handle is a slot index (stable across a resize) plus a
// generation (bumped when a slot is freed), so a handle held past `remove`
// reads as invalid at the next call rather than silently reaching whatever
// node has since reused that slot. Every function below re-derives its own
// `^Slot` from a `Node` immediately before use and never holds one across a
// call into a lifecycle hook — a hook is caller code and is free to
// `create`/`add_child`/`set_parent`/`remove` on this same `Graph`, including
// on the very node it was called for.
//
// ── Lifecycle: hooks, not methods — Dado has neither ────────────────────────
//
// Godot's `_enter_tree`/`_ready`/`_process` become function-ref
// fields a node carries, the same `fn` + `data` dispatch shape `core:jobs`'
// `JobFn` and `app:app`'s `Backend` already use — `userdata` is what lets one
// function serve many node instances (Dado has no closures either), and the
// leading `^Graph` is what lets that same function read or write the very
// node it was called for (there is no other way back to the tree from
// inside a hook — no singleton, no implicit self). Only
// three of Godot's lifecycle notifications are here: `on_enter_tree` /
// `on_exit_tree` fire once, synchronously, inside `add_child`/`remove`;
// `on_ready` fires once, synchronously, immediately after `on_enter_tree`
// — there is no "end of frame" to defer it to the way Godot does, because
// this tree has no frame of its own, only whatever `process` calls it from.
// `_physics_process`/`_input`/`_unhandled_input` are left out: input
// dispatch onto scene nodes is graphical-backend-shaped work
// for a presentation layer, not for this neutral one.
//
// ── The zero-node case: lazy, and no synthetic root ────────────────────────
//
// "Mandatory or lazy" was left for the scene-graph design to decide.
// Settled: **lazy**. `init` allocates nothing; the node pool (`Graph.slots`)
// is only ever `make`d on the first `create`. There is also no single
// mandatory root node — a `Node` attached with an invalid parent simply *is*
// a root, and a `Graph` may hold any number of them side by side. Forcing one
// synthetic root a program never asked for, and would then have to guard
// against removing, buys nothing a pure TUI or immediate-mode program (or a
// server hosting several independent top-level entities) needs — the
// "pay for what you use" applies here exactly as it does to the walking
// skeleton itself.
//
// `destroy` is `init`'s counterpart and the only thing that gives the pool
// back. `remove` returns a node's slot to the free list, which is reuse and
// not release — the run itself is never shrunk — so a `Graph` that outlives
// its owner and is merely dropped leaks every byte `create` ever doubled
// into. A caller that owns a `Graph` for a while (a canvas, a scene, a level)
// needs a verb for the end of that while, and this is it.
//
// ── Where the pool lands ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `init` takes no allocator. It allocates nothing, so there is nothing for one
// to place; what it does is **read the ambient** — `#default`, whatever the
// caller has arranged at that line — and keep it in `Graph.alloc` for the
// `create` that first needs a pool. So the caller decides with one word at the
// call site and this package never carries an allocator through a parameter
// list:
//
//     Graph g = init()                          // wherever the caller's default is
//     Graph g = init() using arena.allocator(&a) // the pool lands in `a`
//
// **`Graph.alloc` survives, and it is not there for `destroy`.** Freeing never
// needed it: `#delete` and `#resize` use the allocator the `ref` itself
// carries, so `destroy` would work with the slot gone. It is there because the
// pool is *lazy* — `alloc_slot` mints it on the first `create`, which is an
// arbitrary distance from `init` — and without the slot that `#make` would
// read whatever ambient happened to be live at that call. A `create` inside a
// scratch frame would then put the node pool in the scratch arena and the
// rollback would take the whole tree with it. Capturing the ambient once, at
// `init`, is what makes the pool's home the same fact it was when this was a
// parameter, and `using` is now how the caller states it.
//
// ── Order is insertion order, in both directions ───────────────────────────
//
// **This changed, and the old behaviour was documented.** `add_child` used
// to push a child onto the front of its parent's list, so sibling order was
// *reverse* insertion order; and the roots were whatever `0..<used` happened
// to yield, which is slot-index order. Both were fine while the only thing
// a walk produced was a side effect. Neither survives a consumer that paints:
// paint order is sibling order, and a program that writes a background and
// then a label on top of it means exactly what it wrote.
//
// So a child is **appended**, and each `Slot` carries a `last_child` to make
// that O(1) rather than a walk to the end — the same 8 bytes per node
// `app:ui`'s own tree already spends for the same reason. And the roots are
// an **explicit ordered list** threaded through the same `next_sibling` field
// the children use, with `first_root`/`last_root` on the `Graph`: no second
// allocation, and the lazy-`init` promise above is kept.
//
// The slot scan had a second defect the order change would not have fixed:
// free-list reuse moves a node's slot index, so removing a root and creating
// another put the new one wherever the free list happened to point. An
// explicit list is stable because nothing but `add_child`/`set_parent`/`remove`
// ever touches it.
//
// One behaviour follows from the list and is worth stating: a node that was
// `create`d and never attached is **not** walked. Under the slot scan it was,
// because "alive with no parent" and "a root" were the same test. They are
// not the same thing — `add_child` is what puts a node in the tree, and it is
// what fires `on_enter_tree` — so a detached node no longer ticks.
//
// ── Presentation properties, carried and never interpreted ─────────────────
//
// `visible`, `modulate`, `self_modulate`, `z_index`, `z_as_relative`, `clip`
// with its custom rect, and `payload` are all stored here and read by nothing
// in this file. That is the point. A renderer built on this tree needs
// somewhere to put them, and the alternatives are a parallel array it keys by
// `Node` — which then has to be resized, freed and generation-checked in
// lockstep with this one, twice the bookkeeping for the same facts — or a
// second tree, which is two trees to keep in step. Neither is worth the
// backend-neutrality it would buy back, because carrying a `[4]u8` costs this
// package no import and no opinion: `modulate` is four bytes here and a colour
// only where somebody multiplies by it.
//
// **`payload` is deliberately not `userdata`.** The four lifecycle hooks
// already own `userdata` — it is the only thing standing in for a closure —
// so a renderer naming its command list there would be fighting the program's
// own hooks for one field. `payload` is a bare `u32` this package never
// dereferences, which is what lets it name a handle into something this
// package has never heard of.
//
// ── The local transform is a matrix; the TRS triple is a view on it ────────
//
// A `Slot` holds both `xform.Transform2D local` and the
// `(position, rotation, scale)` triple, and the triple's setters rebuild the
// matrix as they write. That is one `from_trs` — one `sin`/`cos` pair — per
// *edit* rather than per node per frame, which is what the walk used to pay;
// a tree where most nodes hold still between frames now does no trigonometry
// at all. `set_transform` writes the matrix directly and decomposes it back
// into the triple so `position`/`rotation`/`scale` keep answering.
//
// The decomposition is lossy exactly where `core:geom/xform` says it is: a
// matrix carrying shear has no TRS triple, so `set_transform` followed by
// `rotation` can answer something the caller did not write. The matrix itself
// is kept verbatim and is what composes, so nothing downstream of
// `global_transform` loses anything — only the three readers do, and they
// were already documented as a decomposition.
//
// ── `process` and `update_transforms` are one walk ─────────────────────────
//
// A renderer must not tick game logic in order to find out where things are.
// So the composition — parent's global times the node's own local, written
// into `Slot.global` — is one traversal with a flag, and `process` and
// `update_transforms` are the two ways to enter it. They are not two walks:
// a second copy of the composition arithmetic is the sort of duplication that
// stays correct for exactly as long as nobody edits one of them.
package appgraph

import "core:geom/xform"

// ── handles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A handle to one node. See the package doc comment for why this is an
// index+generation pair rather than a pointer.
type Node: (i32 index, u32 generation)

// The handle that names "no node" — an invalid parent (a root), an absent
// child, an absent sibling.
const Node INVALID = (-1, 0)

bool valid(Node n):
    return n.index >= 0

// ── lifecycle hooks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each takes the `Graph` it belongs to (there is no implicit self and no
// singleton to reach for, per the package doc comment above — a hook
// that wants to read or write its *own* node's transform, or walk to a
// parent or a sibling, needs the `Graph` to do it with), the node's own
// handle, and the `userdata` it was created with.
type ReadyFn:   void(^Graph, Node, rawptr)
type ProcessFn: void(^Graph, Node, f32, rawptr)
type TreeFn:    void(^Graph, Node, rawptr)      // on_enter_tree / on_exit_tree

// ── storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private type Slot: (
    bool alive,
    u32  generation,
    i32  free_next,              // meaningful only when !alive

    bool attached,               // in a parent's child list, or in the root list
    Node parent,
    Node first_child,
    Node last_child,             // O(1) append, so siblings keep insertion order
    Node next_sibling,           // also threads the root list, for a root

    [2]f32 position,
    f32    rotation,
    [2]f32 scale,
    xform.Transform2D local,     // rebuilt by every setter above, never by the walk
    xform.Transform2D global,    // valid after the tree walk that last covered it

    bool   visible,
    [4]u8  modulate,             // composes down a renderer's tree
    [4]u8  self_modulate,        // this node only
    i32    z_index,
    bool   z_as_relative,
    bool   clip,
    [4]f32 custom_rect,          // x, y, w, h — meaningful to whoever set `clip`
    u32    payload,              // opaque; see the package doc comment

    rawptr    userdata,
    ReadyFn   on_ready,
    ProcessFn on_process,
    TreeFn    on_enter_tree,
    TreeFn    on_exit_tree,
)

// The tree. See the package doc comment for the ownership model, and for why
// the roots are a list rather than whatever a scan of the pool turned up.
type Graph: (
    // Where the lazy node pool goes: the ambient `init` was called under, kept
    // because `alloc_slot` mints the pool long after `init` returns. See "Where
    // the pool lands" in the package doc comment.
    (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data)   alloc,
    ref []Slot slots,
    i32         used,        // high-water mark: slots[0..<used] are meaningful
    i32         free_head,   // -1 when the free list is empty
    Node        first_root,
    Node        last_root,
)

// A fresh, empty graph. Allocates nothing until the first `create`, and the
// `Graph` itself is a value the caller holds — this answers one, it does not
// mint one.
//
// It takes no allocator: it reads the ambient one and remembers it for the
// first `create`, so `init() using a` is how a caller puts the node pool in
// `a`. See "Where the pool lands" in the package doc comment.
Graph init():
    Graph g
    g.alloc = #default
    g.free_head = -1
    // Written out because a zeroed `Node` is `(0, 0)`, and index 0 is a
    // perfectly valid slot — "no node" is -1 and nothing else.
    g.first_root = INVALID
    g.last_root = INVALID
    return g

// Tear the tree down, give the node pool back, and leave `g` in exactly the
// state `init` answers — so a `create` after a `destroy` works on the same
// value, and a second `destroy` is a no-op. That re-entrancy is the same
// promise `shutdown` makes elsewhere in this tree, and it is what lets an
// owner call this on a path it is not sure it has already taken.
//
// **The tree is torn down, not merely freed**, which is the question a caller
// has: every node still *in* the tree leaves it, firing `on_exit_tree` once,
// in `remove`'s order — a node before its children, top-down, the same order
// `add_child` fires `on_enter_tree` in. This is `remove` over each root in
// turn and not a scan of the pool, so a hook sees the same tree, in the same
// shape, that removing that root by hand would have shown it, and is free to
// do what any other `on_exit_tree` may do. A hook that attaches a new root on
// its way out has that root torn down too, in the same pass.
//
// A node that was `create`d and never attached fires nothing, because it
// never entered the tree and `on_exit_tree` would be reporting a departure
// that never happened. Its storage goes with the rest.
//
// **A handle held across this call is refused afterwards** — `g.used` is
// zero, so `slot_at` turns every one of them away. It is refused rather than
// detected: the generations went with the run that carried them, so a `create`
// on the emptied `Graph` mints `(0, 0)` again, and a handle that happened to
// be `(0, 0)` before will address the new node. A handle does not survive the
// `Graph` it names, the same way it does not survive the program.
void destroy(^Graph g):
    for valid(g.first_root):
        Node root = g.first_root
        remove(g, root)
        if g.first_root == root:
            break   // the list did not move; leaving is better than spinning
    #delete(g.slots)
    // The pool's home is carried across, so a `create` after a `destroy` puts
    // the fresh pool where the first one went. `init` reads the ambient, and
    // the ambient here is the caller of `destroy`'s, which is not the same
    // fact — hence the `using`.
    (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) home = g.alloc
    g^ = init() using home

// ── slot access (private: every public function re-derives its own
// pointer immediately before use, per the package doc comment) ───────────

private ^Slot slot_at(^Graph g, Node n):
    if n.index < 0 || n.index >= g.used:
        return nil
    ^Slot s = &g.slots[n.index]
    if !s.alive || s.generation != n.generation:
        return nil
    return s

private i32 alloc_slot(^Graph g):
    if g.free_head >= 0:
        i32 idx = g.free_head
        g.free_head = g.slots[idx].free_next
        return idx
    if g.used >= #len(g.slots):
        i64 newcap = #len(g.slots)
        if newcap == 0:
            newcap = 8
        else:
            newcap = newcap * 2
        if g.slots == nil:
            g.slots = #make([]Slot, newcap, g.alloc)
        else:
            bool grew = #resize(g.slots, newcap)
            #assert(grew, "app:graph: out of memory growing the node pool")
    i32 idx = g.used
    g.used = g.used + 1
    return idx

private void free_slot(^Graph g, i32 idx):
    ^Slot s = &g.slots[idx]
    s.alive = false
    s.generation = s.generation + 1
    s.free_next = g.free_head
    g.free_head = idx

// ── creating nodes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Create a detached node — not yet part of the tree, and so not walked by
// `process` or `update_transforms`. `add_child` attaches it, which is what
// fires `on_enter_tree`/`on_ready`. Set hooks/`userdata` and the presentation
// properties with the setters below before `add_child`, if the node needs
// them — **not** constructor parameters here, because a parameter default
// must be a compile-time constant, and `nil` is not one for a pointer
// or function-ref type (confirmed against the compiler: `T fn = nil` as a
// default is refused, ERR0901, "nil… is not something the compiler can
// evaluate," even though an *ordinary statement* assigning `nil` to the
// same field, below, compiles fine — the restriction is specific to a
// folded default, not to `nil` itself).
Node create(^Graph g, [2]f32 position = (0.0, 0.0), f32 rotation = 0.0,
            [2]f32 scale = (1.0, 1.0)):
    i32 idx = alloc_slot(g)
    ^Slot s = &g.slots[idx]
    s.alive = true
    s.attached = false
    s.parent = INVALID
    s.first_child = INVALID
    s.last_child = INVALID
    s.next_sibling = INVALID
    s.position = position
    s.rotation = rotation
    s.scale = scale
    s.local = xform.from_trs(position, rotation, scale)
    s.global = s.local
    // Defaults chosen so that a node nobody configures is the node a
    // renderer would draw plainly: shown, untinted, unclipped, on the layer
    // its parent is on. Every one of them is written out for the same reason
    // the hooks below are — a slot recycled from the free list still holds
    // its previous occupant's values, and a reused slot never gets the
    // zero-fill a `make`d one does. An opaque white `modulate` is the
    // identity of whatever multiplies by it, which is what makes "unset"
    // and "set to white" indistinguishable downstream, on purpose.
    s.visible = true
    s.modulate = (255, 255, 255, 255)
    s.self_modulate = (255, 255, 255, 255)
    s.z_index = 0
    s.z_as_relative = true
    s.clip = false
    s.custom_rect = (0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
    s.payload = 0
    s.userdata = nil
    s.on_ready = nil
    s.on_process = nil
    s.on_enter_tree = nil
    s.on_exit_tree = nil
    return (idx, s.generation)

// ── hooks and userdata (set before `add_child`, if the node needs them) ──

void set_userdata(^Graph g, Node n, rawptr ud):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.userdata = ud

void set_on_ready(^Graph g, Node n, ReadyFn fn):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.on_ready = fn

void set_on_process(^Graph g, Node n, ProcessFn fn):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.on_process = fn

void set_on_enter_tree(^Graph g, Node n, TreeFn fn):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.on_enter_tree = fn

void set_on_exit_tree(^Graph g, Node n, TreeFn fn):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.on_exit_tree = fn

// ── tree edits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private void fire_enter_tree_and_ready(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    TreeFn enter = s.on_enter_tree
    rawptr ud = s.userdata
    if enter != nil:
        enter(g, n, ud)
    // Re-derive: the hook above is caller code and may have grown the pool
    // (a resize moves `g.slots`) or freed `n` itself.
    s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    ReadyFn ready = s.on_ready
    ud = s.userdata
    if ready != nil:
        ready(g, n, ud)

// Put a detached `child` at the end of `parent`'s child list, or at the end
// of the root list when `parent == INVALID`. The one place either list is
// grown, so `add_child` and `set_parent` cannot disagree about what "last"
// means. Fires nothing: the hooks belong to entering the tree, and this is
// only the linking half of it.
private void attach(^Graph g, Node parent, Node child):
    ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
    if cs == nil:
        return
    cs.next_sibling = INVALID
    cs.attached = true
    if valid(parent):
        ^Slot ps = slot_at(g, parent)
        if ps == nil:
            return
        cs.parent = parent
        if valid(ps.last_child):
            ^Slot ls = slot_at(g, ps.last_child)
            if ls != nil:
                ls.next_sibling = child
            ps.last_child = child
        else:
            ps.first_child = child
            ps.last_child = child
    else:
        cs.parent = INVALID
        if valid(g.last_root):
            ^Slot ls = slot_at(g, g.last_root)
            if ls != nil:
                ls.next_sibling = child
            g.last_root = child
        else:
            g.first_root = child
            g.last_root = child

// Attach `child` under `parent`, or as a root when `parent == INVALID`,
// **appended after whatever is already there** so that sibling order and root
// order are insertion order — see the package doc comment, which also records
// that this used to prepend. Fires `on_enter_tree` then `on_ready`, in that
// order, once, synchronously (see the package doc comment for why there is no
// deferred-`ready` semantics here). `child` must not already be in the tree;
// `set_parent` is how an attached node moves.
void add_child(^Graph g, Node parent, Node child):
    ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
    #assert(cs != nil, "app:graph.add_child: child handle is not alive")
    #assert(!cs.attached, "app:graph.add_child: child is already in the tree")
    if valid(parent):
        #assert(slot_at(g, parent) != nil, "app:graph.add_child: parent handle is not alive")
    attach(g, parent, child)
    fire_enter_tree_and_ready(g, child)

private void unlink_from_parent(^Graph g, Node parent, Node child):
    ^Slot ps = slot_at(g, parent)
    if ps == nil:
        return
    if ps.first_child == child:
        ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
        if cs == nil:
            ps.first_child = INVALID
            ps.last_child = INVALID
            return
        ps.first_child = cs.next_sibling
        if ps.last_child == child:
            ps.last_child = INVALID
        return
    Node cur = ps.first_child
    for valid(cur):
        ^Slot curs = slot_at(g, cur)
        if curs == nil:
            break
        if curs.next_sibling == child:
            ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
            if cs == nil:
                curs.next_sibling = INVALID
            else:
                curs.next_sibling = cs.next_sibling
            if ps.last_child == child:
                ps.last_child = cur
            return
        cur = curs.next_sibling

// The root list's half of `unlink_from_parent`, walking `first_root` instead
// of a parent's `first_child`. Two functions rather than one over a pair of
// pointers, because the head of one list lives on a `Slot` and the head of
// the other lives on the `Graph`, and Dado has nothing that addresses both.
private void unlink_from_roots(^Graph g, Node n):
    if g.first_root == n:
        ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
        if s == nil:
            g.first_root = INVALID
            g.last_root = INVALID
            return
        g.first_root = s.next_sibling
        if g.last_root == n:
            g.last_root = INVALID
        return
    Node cur = g.first_root
    for valid(cur):
        ^Slot curs = slot_at(g, cur)
        if curs == nil:
            break
        if curs.next_sibling == n:
            ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
            if s == nil:
                curs.next_sibling = INVALID
            else:
                curs.next_sibling = s.next_sibling
            if g.last_root == n:
                g.last_root = cur
            return
        cur = curs.next_sibling

// Take `n` out of whichever list holds it and leave it detached. A no-op on a
// node that was never attached, which is what keeps `remove` cheap for a node
// that was `create`d and then thrown away.
private void detach(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    if !s.attached:
        return
    if valid(s.parent):
        unlink_from_parent(g, s.parent, n)
    else:
        unlink_from_roots(g, n)
    s.parent = INVALID
    s.next_sibling = INVALID
    s.attached = false

// Whether `probe` is `n` itself or sits somewhere above it. Walks parents,
// which is the short direction — a tree's depth rather than its size — and
// is what makes the cycle refusal below cheap enough to do unconditionally.
private bool is_ancestor_or_self(^Graph g, Node probe, Node n):
    Node cur = n
    for valid(cur):
        if cur == probe:
            return true
        ^Slot s = slot_at(g, cur)
        if s == nil:
            return false
        cur = s.parent
    return false

// Move `child` under `parent`, or into the root list when
// `parent == INVALID`, appended at the end the way `add_child` appends.
//
// Answers whether the move happened. The one refusal is the one that would
// corrupt the tree: a `parent` that is `child` itself or sits below it would
// close the parent chain into a ring, and every walk here — the traversal,
// `unlink_from_parent`, `is_ancestor_or_self` — would then run forever. That
// is a question about the caller's own data rather than a broken handle, and
// a caller dragging a node in a tree view can answer it (refuse the drop),
// so it is returned rather than asserted. A dead handle stays an `#assert`,
// because nothing recovers from one.
//
// A `child` that is not yet in the tree is attached, which fires
// `on_enter_tree` and `on_ready` exactly as `add_child` does. One that is
// already in the tree is **moved**, and a move fires nothing: the node never
// left the tree, and firing `on_exit_tree` for a node that is still in it
// would be a lie told to every hook that reads it.
bool set_parent(^Graph g, Node child, Node parent):
    ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
    #assert(cs != nil, "app:graph.set_parent: child handle is not alive")
    if valid(parent):
        #assert(slot_at(g, parent) != nil, "app:graph.set_parent: parent handle is not alive")
        if is_ancestor_or_self(g, child, parent):
            return false
    if !cs.attached:
        add_child(g, parent, child)
        return true
    detach(g, child)
    attach(g, parent, child)
    return true

private void free_subtree(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    TreeFn exit_fn = s.on_exit_tree
    rawptr ud = s.userdata
    if exit_fn != nil:
        exit_fn(g, n, ud)
    s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return   // the hook already removed this node itself
    Node child = s.first_child
    for valid(child):
        ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
        if cs == nil:
            break
        Node next = cs.next_sibling
        free_subtree(g, child)
        child = next
    s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    free_slot(g, n.index)

// Detach `n` from its parent (or from the root list) and free it and its
// whole subtree, firing `on_exit_tree` on every freed node, parent before
// children — consistently top-down, the same order `add_child`'s
// `on_enter_tree` uses, rather than mixing two orderings. Every handle into
// the freed subtree reads as invalid (`slot_at` returns `nil`) from this
// call onward. A no-op on an already-invalid `n`.
void remove(^Graph g, Node n):
    detach(g, n)
    free_subtree(g, n)

// ── the tree walk ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The one traversal. `fire_process` is what `process` and `update_transforms`
// differ by and it is the whole difference between them — see the package doc
// comment for why there is not a second copy of the two lines above it.
private void walk_subtree(^Graph g, Node n, xform.Transform2D parent_global, f32 dt,
                          bool fire_process):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return
    xform.Transform2D global = parent_global * s.local
    s.global = global
    if fire_process:
        ProcessFn fn = s.on_process
        rawptr ud = s.userdata
        if fn != nil:
            fn(g, n, dt, ud)
        // Re-derive: the hook above may have added, removed or reparented
        // nodes (including `n` itself), and may have grown the pool.
        s = slot_at(g, n)
        if s == nil:
            return
    Node child = s.first_child
    for valid(child):
        ^Slot cs = slot_at(g, child)
        if cs == nil:
            break
        Node next = cs.next_sibling
        walk_subtree(g, child, global, dt, fire_process)
        child = next

private void walk_roots(^Graph g, f32 dt, bool fire_process):
    Node root = g.first_root
    for valid(root):
        ^Slot rs = slot_at(g, root)
        if rs == nil:
            break
        Node next = rs.next_sibling
        walk_subtree(g, root, xform.IDENTITY, dt, fire_process)
        root = next

// Walk every root and its descendants in order, recomputing each node's
// global transform (parent's global composed with the node's own local one —
// see `core:geom/xform`) and firing `on_process(n, dt, userdata)`. A node
// added by a hook during this walk is not visited until the *next* `process`
// call — matching `on_ready` firing at `add_child` time rather than
// mid-walk (package doc comment).
void process(^Graph g, f32 dt):
    walk_roots(g, dt, true)

// The same walk with the hooks left alone: every node's global transform is
// brought up to date and **no `on_process` fires**. This is what a renderer
// calls, because finding out where things are must not advance anything: a
// frame that is redrawn twice, or drawn without having been stepped, would
// otherwise tick game logic once per draw.
void update_transforms(^Graph g):
    walk_roots(g, 0.0, false)

// ── reading the tree ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Node parent_of(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return INVALID
    return s.parent

Node first_child_of(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return INVALID
    return s.first_child

Node last_child_of(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return INVALID
    return s.last_child

Node next_sibling_of(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return INVALID
    return s.next_sibling

// The first root, and the root after `n` — the same two readers
// `first_child_of`/`next_sibling_of` are, over the list the package doc
// comment describes. A caller walks roots with these rather than scanning the
// pool, which is the whole point of the list existing.
Node first_root(^Graph g):
    return g.first_root

Node next_root(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil || valid(s.parent):
        return INVALID
    return s.next_sibling

// Whether `n` is in the tree — attached to a parent or standing in the root
// list. A `create`d node is not, until `add_child` or `set_parent` puts it
// there.
bool in_tree(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return false
    return s.attached

// ── local transform (what a program edits) ──────────────────────────────

[2]f32 position(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return (0.0, 0.0)
    return s.position

void set_position(^Graph g, Node n, [2]f32 p):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.position = p
        s.local = xform.from_trs(p, s.rotation, s.scale)

f32 rotation(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return 0.0
    return s.rotation

void set_rotation(^Graph g, Node n, f32 r):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.rotation = r
        s.local = xform.from_trs(s.position, r, s.scale)

[2]f32 scale(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return (1.0, 1.0)
    return s.scale

void set_scale(^Graph g, Node n, [2]f32 sc):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.scale = sc
        s.local = xform.from_trs(s.position, s.rotation, sc)

// The node's own transform, as the matrix everything here composes with.
xform.Transform2D transform(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return xform.IDENTITY
    return s.local

// Set the local transform from a matrix, for a caller that has one — a
// transform read off another node, an inverse, a product — rather than a
// triple to feed `from_trs`. The matrix is stored as written; the triple is
// decomposed out of it so `position`/`rotation`/`scale` keep answering, and
// loses shear exactly where `core:geom/xform`'s own `rotation_of`/`scale_of`
// document that they do. Nothing downstream of `global_transform` is
// affected by that: composition uses the matrix.
void set_transform(^Graph g, Node n, xform.Transform2D t):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.local = t
        s.position = xform.position_of(t)
        s.rotation = xform.rotation_of(t)
        s.scale = xform.scale_of(t)

// ── presentation properties (carried, never read here) ───────────────────
//
// See the package doc comment for why these live on a backend-neutral tree
// at all, and for why `payload` is not `userdata`.

bool visible(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return false
    return s.visible

void set_visible(^Graph g, Node n, bool v):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.visible = v

// The tint a renderer composes down through this node's whole subtree.
[4]u8 modulate(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return (255, 255, 255, 255)
    return s.modulate

void set_modulate(^Graph g, Node n, [4]u8 c):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.modulate = c

// The tint that applies to this node alone and is not handed to its children
// — Godot's own split, kept because "fade this one thing" and "fade this
// whole branch" are different edits and a caller that has only one of them
// ends up multiplying the other back out by hand.
[4]u8 self_modulate(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return (255, 255, 255, 255)
    return s.self_modulate

void set_self_modulate(^Graph g, Node n, [4]u8 c):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.self_modulate = c

i32 z_index(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return 0
    return s.z_index

void set_z_index(^Graph g, Node n, i32 z):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.z_index = z

// Whether `z_index` is read as an offset from the parent's layer (the
// default, and Godot's) or as an absolute one.
bool z_as_relative(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return true
    return s.z_as_relative

void set_z_as_relative(^Graph g, Node n, bool rel):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.z_as_relative = rel

bool clip(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return false
    return s.clip

void set_clip(^Graph g, Node n, bool on):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.clip = on

// The rectangle `clip` clips to, as `(x, y, w, h)`. Stored whether or not
// `clip` is on, so that turning clipping off and on again does not lose the
// rect — the two are set by two different call sites in every consumer
// written so far.
[4]f32 custom_rect(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return (0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
    return s.custom_rect

void set_custom_rect(^Graph g, Node n, [4]f32 r):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.custom_rect = r

// An opaque `u32` this package stores and never interprets — a renderer's
// name for whatever it hangs off a node. Zero on a fresh node, and zero is
// not special here: what it means is the caller's to decide.
u32 payload(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return 0
    return s.payload

void set_payload(^Graph g, Node n, u32 p):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s != nil:
        s.payload = p

// ── global transform (read-only: valid as of the last `process` or
// `update_transforms` call that covered this node) ───────────────────────

xform.Transform2D global_transform(^Graph g, Node n):
    ^Slot s = slot_at(g, n)
    if s == nil:
        return xform.IDENTITY
    return s.global

[2]f32 global_position(^Graph g, Node n):
    return xform.position_of(global_transform(g, n))

// A point in `n`'s local space, expressed in the tree's root space.
[2]f32 to_global(^Graph g, Node n, [2]f32 local_point):
    return xform.transform_point(global_transform(g, n), local_point)

// A point in the tree's root space, expressed in `n`'s local space.
[2]f32 to_local(^Graph g, Node n, [2]f32 global_point):
    xform.Transform2D inv = xform.inverse(global_transform(g, n))
    return xform.transform_point(inv, global_point)
